From 42f07841eb083f8ae40427b301f1ed66ee02028f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 08:28:07 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 33/37] windows: start application processors from the VMM, not
 from a guest halt loop

This backend claimed that "WHP starts every virtual processor runnable, so the
wait has to be expressed as code", parked every AP at a `cli; hlt; jmp`
trampoline at guest physical 0x1000, and left it at that. Nothing then ever
started an AP. The claim was never verified -- the commit that introduced the
trampoline says the bug it fixed was one that "would have shown up the moment a
Windows guest was given more than one vCPU", i.e. it was reasoned about, not
observed -- and the mechanism it describes cannot work in any case: `cli; hlt`
masks exactly the interrupts that were supposed to wake it, so the only thing
that could ever restart such an AP is an INIT/SIPI applied by the hypervisor.

superradcompany/libkrun (Apache-2.0) asserts the opposite, that "WHP's
in-hypervisor local APIC emulation does not start APs itself". Neither
statement is the whole truth. `WHV_EXTENDED_VM_EXITS.X64ApicInitSipiExitTrap`
(bit 6, per the Windows SDK's WinHvPlatformDefs.h) makes it a choice:

  * leave it clear and WHP applies INIT and SIPI itself -- crosvm's
    WhpxSplitIrqChip, QEMU's kernel-irqchip=on path and Microsoft's own openvmm
    all run in-hypervisor LAPIC emulation with the bit clear, contain no
    INIT/SIPI logic at all, and support SMP;
  * set it and the guest's ICR write comes out as an
    X64ApicInitSipiTrap exit on the writing vCPU, and starting the target
    becomes the VMM's job.

The bit is now set whenever the partition has more than one processor, and
`ApStartupRouter` does the job: it decodes the trapped ICR (INIT and startup
delivery modes; self, all-including-self and all-excluding-self shorthands;
physical destination resolved through each processor's actual
`WHvX64RegisterApicId` rather than an assumed identity map), holds each AP's
thread outside `WHvRunVirtualProcessor` until its startup IPI arrives, and then
has that AP's own thread apply the vector.

Taking the trap is what makes this correct without having to settle the one
question the evidence disagrees on. crosvm and QEMU both write a boot state
into every vCPU and run them all immediately, which only works if WHP holds APs
in wait-for-SIPI; openvmm's OpenHCL backend, against the same hypervisor
through a different interface, states the reverse outright and sets the flag
itself. Because an AP's thread never enters the guest before its SIPI, and
because `apply_sipi_startup_state` clears `WHvRegisterInternalActivityState`
(bit 0, `StartupSuspend`) unconditionally, the result is the same either way.

Three properties of the trap are inferred rather than documented, no project
having been found that enables the bit: that the exit lands on the sending
vCPU, that `ApicIcr` carries the two xAPIC ICR halves combined, and that the
instruction has already retired so RIP must not be advanced. The first two fail
visibly and harmlessly -- parked APs and a verbatim warn of the undecodable ICR
-- and the third is bounded by a consecutive-trap counter that fails the vCPU
with a named error instead of letting the sender spin forever.

INIT is handled rather than dropped, which is what lets a guest offline a CPU
and bring it back: an INIT to a running AP re-parks it and cancels its run, and
its thread returns to waiting. The level-triggered INIT de-assert is ignored
explicitly -- Linux sends it with destination 0 and no shorthand, so taken at
face value it reads as "reset the boot processor, mid-boot".

The trampoline stays. It is no longer how an AP waits, but it is still the
state an AP holds while parked, and the difference between four bytes that halt
and zeroed memory that triple-faults is worth keeping for a state whose
reachability is precisely what is not established. It also makes "this AP never
got its SIPI" (rip=0x1001) legible next to "this AP started and crashed".

A host whose WHP does not offer the trap now fails partition setup with a named
error for vcpus > 1 instead of booting a guest whose APs can never start.

Adapted in part from superradcompany/libkrun (Apache-2.0), whose WHP backend
implements the same trap-based design.

x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu and aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu,
-p libkrun --features blk,net: 0 errors each. `cargo fmt --all --check` clean.
NOT RUN AGAINST A GUEST: there is no Windows host, no WHP and no VM in this
environment, and no part of this has executed.

